{
  "analysis": "Kept model (30/10/10, default scale) = 0.5746; inclusion (0.329) is the bottleneck, misses are small (median 33.8px vs 90.5px train). Round 1 showed small scale helped inclusion only in the balanced setting (+0.031) but under inclusion-heavy allocation the all-small combo gave inclusion 0.3163 — so smallness under heavy allocation is genuinely uncertain. Scratches paid ~0.04 for the inclusion-heavy allocation (0.643→0.602). This round I isolate one factor per candidate vs the control (30/10/10 default): (1) match inclusion source size to the missed distribution via prefer_small weighting (no downscaling artifacts), (2) recover scratches via any_class backgrounds (lesson probe), (3) clean isolation of inclusion small scale 0.3-0.7 with scratches/patches at default. If C1/C3 both fail, smallness is ruled out for round 3 and I push allocation/backgrounds instead.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Source-size-matching hypothesis: missed inclusions are small (median 33.8px) while train sources average 90.5px; weighting smaller inclusion sources (prefer_small) at default scale should shift the paste size distribution toward the missed range without the downscaling artifacts that may have hurt the round-1 small-scale combo. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 > 0.335 and mAP50 > 0.5746 with scratches holding >= 0.59.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Scratches background-diversity hypothesis: the inclusion-heavy allocation cost scratches ~0.04 AP50 (0.643 balanced vs 0.602 heavy); pasting scratches onto any_class backgrounds (cleaner/more varied contexts) should recover part of that loss without giving up inclusion allocation. Confirmed if scratches AP50 >= 0.62 with inclusion AP50 unchanged (~0.33) and mAP50 > 0.5746.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "any_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion small-scale isolation (uncertain alternative): round 1 showed small scale helped inclusion in the balanced setting but the all-small heavy-allocation combo was worse; this tests small scale for inclusion ONLY (0.3-0.7, same as round 1) with patches/scratches at default. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 > 0.335 and low-tertile recall rises; if it ties/loses to control, smallness is ruled out for inclusion under heavy allocation and round 3 should focus on allocation/backgrounds.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Control (4, repeat 30/10/10 default) jumped to 0.6334 (+0.059) purely from cumulative synthetic — the default inclusion-heavy recipe compounds. Candidate 3 (small-scale inclusion 0.3-0.7x) hit 0.6303 with the BEST inclusion/worst-class AP50 (0.493, +0.164 vs before) and best scratches (0.7023), only -0.003 mAP50 vs control (within noise) but a real patches dip (-0.085). Candidate 1 (prefer_small inclusion sources) was a big net loss (0.5788, -0.055 vs control): small inclusion SOURCES are low-contrast → more FPs, so downscaling large sources beats using small sources. Candidate 2 (any_class scratch backgrounds) also a net loss (0.5696, -0.064): scratches rose (+0.040) but patches collapsed (-0.070). Candidates 3 and 4 are within 0.005 mAP50, so the tie-break (higher worst-class AP50) selects candidate 3 (0.493 > 0.4568).",
  "choice": 3,
  "lesson": "Cumulative default inclusion-heavy synthetic compounds strongly (control +0.059 just from adding more). Small-scale inclusion (0.3-0.7x downscaling of large sources) is the real lever: best inclusion/worst-class AP50 (0.493) at ~no mAP50 cost — KEEP it; but prefer_small inclusion SOURCES and any_class scratch backgrounds are both big net losses (mAP50 -0.05/-0.06 vs control), so avoid them. Round 3: build on small-scale inclusion and try to recover the patches dip (-0.085 vs control) via a bit more patches allocation or default-scale patches, while confirming small-scale inclusion holds."
}
